"Lonely unmarried looking for love, life was passing me by

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:56.340
"So I sent off my photo hobbies and age, magazine marriage I tried

00:01:56.340 --> 00:02:01.120
"They say for centuries lovely Japanese girls have been trained in the art of pleasing men

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:05.420
"Be lonely no more open destiny's door for one dollar they arrange a meeting"

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:10.940
For 10 years the brilliant Craig Ferguson has lit a fire from within the late-night talk-show universe,

00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:13.840
Systematically deconstructing all its elements,

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:17.380
whether it's the deferential sidekick, the live band or the pre-interview

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:21.860
"In this show more than any, the pre-interview means nothing. "

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:26.160
"It means nothing, it's meaningless. The pre-interview is a formality like wearing a tie or underpants. "

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:29.680
Indeed it seemed like the biggest enemy to Ferguson sense of comedy

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:32.100
was the very foundation of late-night TV-

00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:33.540
structure itself.

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:35.080
"Who's that at the door? "

00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:44.420
Ferguson actively sought out the unrehearsed, the spontaneous.

00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:47.300
He encouraged the awkward the weird the difficult .

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:49.880
"You know, we should start rehearsing this show. "

00:02:49.880 --> 00:02:53.660
He'd recognize and despise late-nights' deification of celebrity

00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:57.080
and took every opportunity to mock us for buying into them.

00:02:57.080 --> 00:03:00.760
"It's a short time on this planet, man. It's a date and then a hyphen and then another date.

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:02.780
"And we are the hyphen, and I'm like,

00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:07.040
"I'm not spending the whole time talking to [beep] celebrities, man. I'm not doing it. "

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:11.900
Now this isn't to say that hosts like Jimmy Fallon, who I do like, is somehow bankrupt

00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:17.580
because all he does is pay homage to the format by putting it in terms young people of today can understand.

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:21.980
It's just that he and Ferguson sit at opposite ends of the spectrum.

00:03:21.980 --> 00:03:27.640
Or rather Ferguson more than anyone else willingly places himself outside the spectrum

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:33.220
and his relationship to late-night is of an altogether different quality, a revolutionary one.

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:39.860
"What gives you the right to let Americans not hear cuss words? You- [beep]

00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:41.540
"What gives you the right? "

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:46.900
Indeed the tension between Ferguson and late-night was what made Ferguson so funny.

00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:51.020
His ability to express contempt for his position, his network, his job,

00:03:51.020 --> 00:03:56.580
his willingness to admit his failures, to dwell on them, was for many of us, extremely liberating.

00:03:56.600 --> 00:03:59.680
And that's because late-night TV is a fantasy,

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:02.460
a fantasy of sophistication and charm and fun,

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:06.660
a standard to which, after enough time, we hold ourselves unwittingly.

00:04:06.680 --> 00:04:08.900
No one knew this better than craig Ferguson.

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:12.560
For 10 fucking awesome fucking weird years,

00:04:12.580 --> 00:04:17.780
he tried to show us that life bears far less resemblance to a string of funny stories

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:21.940
than to the long awkward pauses that exist between them.

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:52.760
Well, I hope we've done is- is-

00:04:52.760 --> 00:04:56.380
is maybe trying to- maybe art is a very grand word,

00:04:56.380 --> 00:04:59.880
but- but- but what I was trying to do here

00:04:59.980 --> 00:05:04.760
and what I think that we've managed to do here is make something that wasn't here before.

